2.	Explain the difference between Multi-AZ and Multi-Region architecture.
multiple Availability Zones	multiple AWS Regions
Multi-AZ means deploying infrastructure across multiple Availability Zones within the same AWS Region.
Region: ap-south-1 (Mumbai) -> 1(a), 1(b), 1(c)
Main goal:
•	High Availability 
•	protection against AZ/datacentre failure	Multi-Region means deploying the application across multiple AWS Regions.
Region:
Primary Region → ap-south-1 (Mumbai)
Secondary Region → us-east-1 (Virginia)
Main goals:
•	Disaster Recovery (DR) 
•	Global availability 
•	Low latency for global users 
•	Business continuity during regional outages
If Mumbai region goes down completely: Route53 failover redirects traffic to Virginia.


Multi-AZ architecture improves high availability within a single AWS region by distributing workloads across multiple Availability Zones. 
Multi-Region architecture extends resilience further by deploying workloads across geographically separate AWS regions, primarily for disaster recovery, global scalability, and protection against complete regional failures. 
Multi-AZ is generally the default for production workloads, while multi-Region is used for mission-critical systems requiring maximum resilience.”
Important Interview Point: Multi-AZ ≠ Disaster Recovery
Many candidates incorrectly say multi-AZ is DR; Correct answer is:
•	Multi-AZ provides High Availability 
•	Multi-Region provides Disaster Recovery
3.	How do you design fault-tolerant systems in AWS?

To design fault-tolerant systems in AWS, I eliminate single points of failure by distributing workloads across multiple Availability Zones and, where required, multiple regions. I use load balancers, Auto Scaling, Kubernetes self-healing, managed databases with failover, asynchronous messaging, Infrastructure as Code, and comprehensive monitoring. The goal is to ensure the system continues operating automatically even during infrastructure, application, or network failures.
