4.	How would you architect a scalable microservices platform on AWS?
I would design a scalable microservices platform on AWS using containerized services deployed on EKS, with each service independently scalable and owning its own database. Communication would be a mix of synchronous APIs via ALB/API Gateway and asynchronous messaging using SQS, SNS, and EventBridge. The system would be highly available using multi-AZ deployment, autoscaling at both pod and node levels, and fault isolation through decoupling. CI/CD pipelines with Jenkins or GitHub Actions and Terraform for infrastructure ensure repeatability and fast delivery, while observability using Prometheus, Grafana, and CloudWatch ensures operational visibility.

5.	What services would you use for a production-grade CI/CD pipeline?
For a production-grade CI/CD pipeline, I would use GitHub as source control, and Jenkins or GitHub Actions for CI. 
The pipeline would build and test the application, run security scans, and build a Docker image which is pushed to Amazon ECR. 
For deployment, I would use Kubernetes on EKS with Helm (or ArgoCD for GitOps-based) deployments.
Terraform would manage infrastructure provisioning. 
The pipeline would promote the same artifact from dev to staging to production with approval gates, and production deployments would use blue/green (or canary strategies).
Monitoring would be handled using CloudWatch, Prometheus, and Grafana with automated rollback triggered by health metrics.
6.	Explain how you would separate environments (dev/stage/prod) in AWS.
I separate environments primarily using AWS multi-account strategy under AWS Organizations, which gives strong security boundaries.
Each environment has its own VPC, compute, database, and storage resources. 
CI/CD pipelines promote the same build artifact from dev to staging to production with appropriate approvals. 
Terraform ensures consistent infrastructure across environments, while IAM policies and SCPs (Service Control Policy) enforce strict governance, especially in production.
SCP = Service Control Policy (AWS Organizations feature)
SCPs are account-level guardrails used in AWS Organizations to control:
“What actions are allowed or denied inside an AWS account — regardless of IAM permissions.”
So even if a developer has admin IAM access, SCP can still block actions at the account level.

7.	How would you design a centralized logging architecture?
I would design a centralized logging architecture using Fluent Bit in Kubernetes and CloudWatch agents on EC2 to collect logs from all services.
Logs would be shipped to Amazon CloudWatch Logs for real-time monitoring and optionally to OpenSearch for advanced search and visualization.
For long-term retention, logs would be archived in S3 with lifecycle policies.
I would enforce structured JSON logging with correlation IDs to trace requests across microservices.
Alerts would be generated using CloudWatch metric filters and routed via SNS to Slack or PagerDuty.
This design ensures centralized visibility, scalability, security, and efficient debugging across distributed systems.
