5. Explain active-active vs active-passive setup.
In an Active-Active setup:
Multiple environments/regions are active simultaneously and serve live traffic at the same time.

Both regions:
•	process requests 
•	handle traffic 
•	remain operational simultaneously	In an Active-Passive setup:
One environment actively serves traffic, while another standby environment waits for failover.

If primary region fails:
•	Route53 health check detects failure 
•	traffic redirects to standby region

Active-active architecture runs multiple regions simultaneously to serve production traffic, providing maximum availability, scalability, and low latency, but with higher complexity and cost. Active-passive architecture uses one primary environment and a standby environment for disaster recovery, making it simpler and more cost-effective, though failover times are slightly higher. The choice depends on business SLA, RTO/RPO requirements, global traffic patterns, and operational complexity tolerance.
