9. Cost Optimization Architecture

1.	How would you optimize AWS infrastructure costs?

Analyse Current Usage 
•	Use AWS Cost Explorer, AWS Budgets, and AWS Trusted Advisor to identify high-cost services, under-utilized resources, and spending trends. 
•	Tag resources properly to track costs by project, environment, or team. 
Right-Size Resources 
•	Review CPU, memory, and storage utilization using Amazon CloudWatch. 
•	Downgrade overprovisioned EC2 instances and choose appropriate instance families based on workload requirements. 
Eg: An instance is tagged to type of x – which has (high CPU, large memory), observe for few weeks and downgrade to a y (medium CPU, medium memory) depending on the actual load.

Use Auto Scaling 
•	Configure Auto Scaling groups so resources scale up during peak demand and scale down during low-usage periods. 
•	This prevents paying for unused capacity. 
Leverage Cost-Effective Pricing Models 
•	Use Reserved Instances or Savings Plans for predictable workloads to reduce costs significantly. 
•	Use Spot Instances for fault-tolerant or batch-processing workloads where interruptions are acceptable. 
Optimize Storage 
•	Move infrequently accessed data to cheaper storage classes such as S3 Standard-IA, S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, or S3 Glacier Deep Archive. 
•	Delete obsolete snapshots, unused EBS volumes, and old backups. 
Reduce Data Transfer Costs 
•	Use CloudFront for content delivery. 
•	Keep services within the same Availability Zone or Region, when possible, to minimize inter-region and cross-AZ transfer charges. 
Automate Resource Scheduling 
•	Automatically stop non-production environments during nights and weekends using AWS Lambda or Instance Scheduler. 
Monitor and Govern Costs 
•	Set up AWS Budgets and cost alerts. 
•	Regularly review cost reports and establish FinOps practices to ensure ongoing optimization.

2.	What is AWS Cost Explorer?

AWS Cost Explorer is a cost management tool provided by AWS that helps you analyze, visualize, and understand your AWS spending and usage patterns.
It provides graphs and reports that show:
•	How much you're spending on AWS 
•	Which services are generating the highest costs 
•	Cost trends over time (daily, monthly, yearly) 
•	Spending by account, region, service, tag, or resource 
•	Forecasted future costs

3.	How AWS Budget Alerts Are Set?

1.	Go to AWS Billing and Cost Management. 
2.	Open AWS Budgets. 
3.	Click Create Budget. 
4.	Select Cost Budget. 
5.	Enter the budget amount (for example, $10,000/month). 
6.	Configure alert thresholds such as: 
o	50% of budget ($5,000) 
o	80% of budget ($8,000) 
o	100% of budget ($10,000) 
7.	Choose notification methods: 
o	Email 
o	Amazon SNS Topic (for integration with Slack, Lambda, etc.) 
8.	Save the budget.

Note:
Many organizations create:
•	Actual Cost Alerts → Trigger when money is already spent. 
•	Forecasted Cost Alerts → Trigger when AWS predicts the budget will be exceeded before the end of the month.


